{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Binding to the vine, etc.</b> Many have interpreted this verse, and all of them interpreted by way of derash. Perhaps it further intends to speak of the coming of King Mashiach, which is mentioned just above: ״עַד כִּי יָבֹא שִׁילֹה״ and so forth. And it says ״וְלוֹ יִקְּהַת עַמִּים״ — that to him peoples will inquire. He said that he will bind in chains and imprisonment before the vine — which is Israel, who are likened to a vine, as it is written (Psalms 80:9): ״גֶּפֶן מִמִּצְרַיִם תַּסִּיעַ״ — he will bind before them עִירֹה, meaning all the nations who are likened to a donkey, as it is written (Ezekiel 23:20): ״אֲשֶׁר בְּשַׂר חֲמוֹרִים בְּשָׂרָם״, and so our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Kiddushin 68a) on the verse ״עִם הַחֲמוֹר״ — a people likened to a donkey. ״וְלַשּׂוֹרֵקָה״ — meaning, before one branch of the vine called sorek, he will also bind his donkey's colt. And he was precise to say עַיִר and אָתוֹן because there is, within the aspect of the kelippah which is the root of the souls of the nations, an aspect of male and female; and the male aspect is called עַיִר and the female aspect is called אָתוֹן. And corresponding to the vine, which is the totality of Israel, he said ״אֹסְרִי עִירֹה״ — which is the male aspect, which is the power of the nations; and corresponding to one branch of Israel he said ״אֲתוֹנוֹ״, to say that regarding the female aspect he will not need all of Israel but through the mediation of one branch it suffices." "<b>Or</b> it may say on this path, that the verse speaks of the two well-known redemptions: the first is that of Egypt, and the second is the future redemption with HaShem's help. Corresponding to the first redemption he said <b>אֹסְרִי לַגֶּפֶן</b>, along the lines of what it says ״גֶּפֶן מִמִּצְרַיִם תַּסִּיעַ״ (Psalms 80:9) — for then HaShem subjugated before them one aspect called עַיִר, and it was humbled before the vine. And corresponding to the second redemption with HaShem's help that will be in our days, he said <b>וְלַשּׂוֹרֵקָה</b> — meaning, at the time when HaShem will whistle, as it is written (Zechariah 10:8): ״אֶשְׁרְקָה לָהֶם וַאֲקַבְּצֵם״, then ״בְּנִי אֲתוֹנוֹ״ — said in the plural, meaning every aspect of the nations; for in the Exodus from Egypt, HaShem humbled before them only the head of the nations, which are Egypt and Canaan, which are the seven nations, who were the head and the might of all the nations; but the seventy nations were not humbled and were not given to them. But in the future, with HaShem's help, He will not leave from the nations a great nation or a small one that will not be given to them, and this is what he said <b>בְּנִי אֲתֹנוֹ</b> — meaning, besides עִירֹה, also the offspring of the donkey." "And perhaps we may further say, along the lines of what they say in the Zohar (Pinḥas 231a), that in the sitra aḥra the female is stronger than the male, for the male takes the yotertet of hers and it is called the yotertet of the liver (Leviticus 9:10, Leviticus 3:15) — and similar to this matter. And in a vision shown to me in a dream, that I wrestled in battle with the male, and it was light in my eyes to subdue him, and I cast him down with my strength; but with the female I exerted all my strength many times, and after many labors I was able to throw her several degrees down to the ground — but not like her master the male. And therefore he said, corresponding to the future redemption, ״וְלַשּׂוֹרֵקָה״ — the time when HaShem will whistle and gather them — ״בְּנִי אֲתֹנוֹ״ — those who come from the power of the female, them too HaShem will bind before the children of Israel." "And let it not be difficult in your eyes that we divide the words of the verse, part in the days of Moses and part in the days of Mashiach — for do you not know the words of the holy Zohar (vol. II 120a), that Moses is the redeemer who redeemed our forefathers; he will redeem us and restore children to their border, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 1:9): ״מַה שֶּׁהָיָה הוּא שֶׁיִּהְיֶה״ — the initial letters spell Moshe. And let this not be difficult in your eyes when you say: is King Mashiach not from the tribe of Judah, from the seed of King David, upon him be peace? And some say (Sanhedrin 98b) that David himself is King Mashiach, as it is written (Ezekiel 37:24): ״וְעַבְדִּי דָוִד מֶלֶךְ עֲלֵיהֶם״ — according to its plain meaning; and if so, how can we say that he is Moses, who comes from the tribe of Levi?" "<b>You should</b> know that the aspect of the soul of Moses, upon him be peace, is included from the twelve tribes of Israel, for all sixty myriads were its branches upon him be peace, and the branch of the tribe of David is within Moses. And therefore you find him in the wilderness that he was king and priest and Levite and prophet and sage and mighty warrior — for he included all the branches within holiness. And in the future there will be revealed in the world the root of kingship that is within Moses — that he himself is King Mashiach, and he is David, and he is Yinnon, and he is Shiloh." "<b>He washes in wine</b>, etc. This is explained according to the words of the sages of blessed memory (Zohar Chadash, parashat Bereshit), that the fourth exile, in which the mighty redeemer will be revealed, requires that the mitzvah of engaging in Torah be in Israel's hand, and without this his coming will be delayed. And to this the verse alludes by saying <b>He washes</b>, etc. — meaning that the power of the awaited king whose name is Shiloh is the aspect of engagement in Torah, that Israel will engage in the wine of Torah. And see what they expounded on the verse (Shir HaShirim Rabbah, ch. 2): ״הֱבִיאַנִי אֶל בֵּית הַיָּיִן״ — and they said that through its agency his garment, which is a designation for kingship, will be fittingly donned by him." "And its saying <b>and in the blood of grapes</b>, etc., intends to say that if the time of redemption arrives and no wine is found — meaning, there is no Torah among us — the redemption will come about through the yoke and pressure of the exile which the nations inflicted upon Israel, as they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 97b): when the designated end arrives and there is no merit in Israel to redeem them, He will set over them a king as harsh as Haman, etc. And this is its saying <b>and in the blood of grapes</b> — for through suffering the souls will be refined and the sparks of holiness will be purified, just as they are purified through the aspect of Torah, except that this one — its work is beautiful, and that one — its work is not beautiful." "And its saying <b>grapes</b> — for through suffering her preparation will be completed, like the completion of the ripening of the fruit of the vine, which are the grapes. And he also alluded that the nations will not have dominion to shed blood for a vine-branch or a shoot or a cluster, but rather grapes — meaning, individual berries." "And he said <b>his garment</b> (סוּתֹה) to allude to a lower level than the covering (כְּסוּת), for סוּת means the language of necessary covering — the language of a garment. And the intention in this is that there will be a great difference between whether the redemption comes about through Torah and mitzvot — then it will be revealed in the revelation of supernal kingship — and its coming about through the vengeance that will be enacted in Israel, which will be in the aspect of עָנִי וְרוֹכֵב — a poor man riding, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:08.649177420Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}